**PR body:**
Captures the architectural decision made on 2026-06-12 after the Halo SDK was published (https://docs.brilliant.xyz/halo/halo-sdk/) and Brilliant Labs sent the "We have PCBs!" notification (Foxconn QA week of 2026-06-15, production kick-off week of 21st or 28th). The SDK supports four languages (Python, Flutter, Web Bluetooth as host languages; Lua on-device); this ADR documents which combination compass uses and why, plus the deferral path for phone mobility. ADR 0008 (Halo primary, Frame backup, no committed fallback) remains in force; this ADR documents the *implementation strategy* once hardware arrives, not the hardware choice itself.

## Background

The published Halo SDK (https://github.com/brilliantlabsAR/brilliant_sdk + https://docs.brilliant.xyz/halo/halo-sdk/) exposes four programming layers:

1. **Lua on-device.** Code uploaded to Halo's MCU. Runs locally, no host needed for reflexes (button events, immediate HUD updates, animations). Subsecond latency.
2. **Python host.** `brilliant-msg` (PyPI, BSD-licensed, "OS Independent"). Communicates with Halo over BLE from a desktop/laptop/server. Drives camera, mic, speaker, display, IMU. This is the heavy-lifting layer.
3. **Flutter host.** First-class mobile path (iOS + Android). For self-contained phone apps.
4. **Web Bluetooth host.** Chromium-only browser-based path. For demos and zero-install sharing.

The Ethos-U55 NPU (~46 GOPS) on Halo is not exposed to third-party developer models in the current SDK and would not run a general VLM regardless of access. Compass's cloud-VLM design (Claude Sonnet 4.6) remains correct.

Compass's existing architecture is 100% Python with structural typing via `typing.Protocol`. The natural fit is Python host + minimal Lua on-device. Flutter and Web Bluetooth solve a different problem (self-contained mobile / no-install demos) and would require partial or full rewrites that aren't justified by V0-V1 use cases.

The user has explicitly stated long-term interest in phone-based mobility ("so I can take it anywhere and interact with other resources"). The cleanest architectural path to phone mobility from Python is **phone-as-BLE-bridge to cloud Python** — the phone maintains the BLE connection to Halo and shuttles events to a Python server, while all heavy lifting (Whisper STT, Claude calls, memory) stays on the server. This preserves the existing Python codebase and is added when use cases demand it, not speculatively.

